not need to tell us which one you chose.


WHY TWO
-------

Both are permissive: use, modify, redistribute, sell, or build something closed on
top of this, without owing us anything. They differ in one respect that matters to
some organisations:

  * Apache-2.0 includes an EXPRESS PATENT GRANT. Contributors grant you a licence to
    any of their patents that the software necessarily infringes, and that grant
    terminates only if you initiate patent litigation over the software. Corporate
    legal review often asks for this.

  * MIT is shorter and simpler, and is silent on patents.

Offering both means neither preference blocks adoption. This mirrors the convention
of the Rust ecosystem, which J.H AI Agent depends on heavily.


WHAT THIS COVERS
----------------

The above applies to the source code in this repository.

Third-party components redistributed here — the tree-sitter WebAssembly binaries
under public/tree-sitter/, and the Google protocol-buffer definitions under
.proto_include/ — remain under their own licences and carry their own copyright
notices. See THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.

Dependencies fetched at build time by npm and cargo are not covered by this file and
remain under their own licences.
